# Target C — stress-star reciprocal-perimeter frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

Full proof: [target_c_stress_star_force_perimeter_2026-08-31.md](../../reports/target_c_stress_star_force_perimeter_2026-08-31.md).

## C69 — star mass separates the reciprocal perimeter from its lower endpoint

For a diameter vertex `z`, let

`S_z=sum_(e incident z) beta_e`.

The C14 sixty-degree diameter cone gives the generic local reaction bound

`|g_b| >= (sqrt(3)/2) D s_b`.

A neighbor `k` of `z` has the stronger bound

`|g_k| >= D(beta_zk + (s_k-beta_zk)/2)`,

because every other diameter vector at `k` makes angle at most 60 degrees with the star vector `k-z`.

Summing the center, star neighbors, and all remaining diameter endpoints gives the exact estimate

`P_*/D >= 1 + (sqrt(3)/2) S_z`.

C66 supplies a star center with

`S_z >= 1-(2pi/kappa_0)D epsilon`.

Therefore

`P_* >= (1+sqrt(3)/2)D - (sqrt(3)pi/kappa_0)D^2 epsilon`.

In particular, if `D epsilon ->0`, then

`liminf P_*/D >= 1+sqrt(3)/2 = 1.8660254037...`.

Thus the surviving stress-star phase is separated by a fixed amount from C14's lower reciprocal-perimeter endpoint `sqrt(3)D`. It still lies below the universal upper endpoint `2D`, so this is not itself a contradiction.

## Next target

C68 remains the sharper mechanical route. Combine its radial angular-energy defect with near-quadrangulation or another source-level condition to prevent the remaining high-perimeter, highly anisotropic unit-contact carrier.
